00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.429 align:start position:0%
 
we start with the latest efforts to  defuse growing tensions between russia  and ukraine ukraine's president vladimir  celenski is calling for calm after  speaking with u.s president biden he  said there has been no further  escalation with russia at the moment and  he is asking allies to dial down talk of  war  earlier russian president putin whose  country has masked tens of thousands of  troops near ukraine's border accused  nato and the u.s of ignoring russia's  security concerns nato says it is  prepared to put more troops in eastern  europe after washington warned ukraine  to be on alert for an invasion  scanning the landscape on this ukrainian  border city  troops try to gauge moscow's next move  one soldier believes an attack here in  luhansk is unlikely  our navy is not strong enough to  withstand heavy attack  the coastline could be easily captured  that's my opinion  everybody thinks about invasion through  haki for donbass but that's the wrong  point of view  if they attack they will do it from the  sea  in the black sea russia's navy carries  out drills on friday  still the kremlin says it wants a  peaceful resolution  if it's up to us the russian federation  there will be no war  we don't want war but we will not allow  interest to be violated  and we will not allow our interests to  be ignored either  it's hoped a diplomatic offensive will  de-escalate tensions high-stakes talks  with various players are giving hope  that a war in eastern europe can be  averted  for those stuck in the middle  life is on  we hold hope russia takes us for good  because it has to stop at some point  it's been what  eight years of conflict  as diplomatic efforts advance the u.s is  still warning ukraine to prepare for a  russian invasion  for more on the story we can speak now  to radoswafski who played a key role in  formulating the eu's response to russia  when he served as polish foreign  minister during the ukraine crisis in  2014 and mr cerkorski is now part of the  conservative bloc in the european  parliament welcome to dw and good  evening to you  if we look at what ukraine's president  has been saying today he is essentially  saying that the west and journalists in  particular are playing up this panic  then it's time to dial down uh the  tensions now the u.s says a russian  attack is distinctly possible next month  what do you think moscow is planning  well president zielenski needs to keep  his economy his the finances of ukraine  uh stable so he needs to reassure people  but we know from satellite imagery that  over a hundred thousand troops  uh are massed uh uh all around ukraine  ukraine is surrounded on three sides by  russia  um that this has been exercised before  and that russia is making demands  president putin regards ukraine as a  temporary  artificial stage and he's made it very  plain repeatedly i was at the nato  summit in bucharest in 2008  when he spoke about it quite openly  and he last july he published a  manifesto for the subjugation or  dismembering  of ukraine so this is very serious oh so  russia has of course on the record  denied any plans to invade ukraine but  why do you think we're seeing this troop  build up now what prompted it is it do  you think as some analysts have  suggested that  vladimir putin is taking advantage of a  time where europe is distracted  well of course  you know that the russians lied about  not wanting to take over crimea either  and then they lied again about donbass  and they lied about the malaysian  airliner  and so on uh so yes i mean aggressors  lie nothing new about that um  but why do they have a hundred thousand  troops massed  uh ukraine is a democracy a a member of  the u.n in good standing uh a member of  the osce uh ukraine has russian security  guarantees  uh budapest memorandum of 1994 under  which ukraine gave up what was then the  third largest nuclear arsenal in return  for these guarantees which which russia  has already broken well i have to ask  you 