e neighbor i remember  his  you know  germany um wreaked a huge destruction uh  to ukraine mainly in belarus not russia  during the second world war you have a  special duty to preserve the peace in  europe  vader schwab  a former polish foreign minister joining  us here on our program thank you so much  so much for sharing your insights with  us today  my pleasure  with the focus uh on ukraine at the  moment germany and its ties with russia  are also in the spotlight and we can  bring in our political correspondent  thomas sparrow for the latest on that hi  thomas a lot of questions are being  asked here in germany about what the  country is willing to put on the table  so is germany prepared to suffer  economic consequences to send a message  to russia  it is certainly preparing itself to  suffer economic consequences and in fact  germany's foreign minister alina berbock  stressed precisely that very recently  when she met u.s secretary of state  anthony blinken saying that any  aggression from russia will be met with  strong consequences from the west even  if that means also economic consequences  at home in particular what everyone is  talking about is the north stream two  pipeliner pipeline which has already  been built under the baltic sea joining  russia and germany which is waiting for  regulatory approval and which is being  strongly criticized by countries like  the united states or some of germany's  allies in the european union including  for example  poland whereas german officials in the  current government but also in previous  governments have stressed that nordstrom  2 is mostly an economic project the us  and these european countries believe  that it is putting germany at risk  making europe vulnerable and giving too  much power to russia  so that's why in essence nordstrom 2 is  becoming a very important and also very  controversial part of this whole  equation around the us russia ukraine  conflict and so on and thomas i want to  ask you about another part of the debate  here in germany which is whether to send  arms to ukraine why is it that germany  is still refusing to send weapons  and this is one issue sumi where germany  differs from other countries whereas the  us or the uk have sent defensive weapons  to ukraine germany has refused to do so  they did send for example 5 000 helmets  to ukraine but they are not willing to  send defensive weapons essentially  because they believe  that that could make tensions even  higher and that could make the prospect  of a negotiated solution even more  difficult to achieve and it's important  to stress that germany alongside other  countries like france is really hoping  for a negotiated solution to all of this  for example by reviving the so-called  normandy format talks france germany  russia and ukraine so germany's hope is  essentially put on a diplomatic solution  to the crisis and not in sending  defensive weapons to ukraine  our political correspondent thomas  sparrow thanks so much for your analysis  you

